#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  EUDI Verified Call — configuration
#
#  Copy this file to `.env` and fill in section 1. Everything else is optional.
#
#  ┌─ 1. REQUIRED ────────────── without these the server will not start in production
#  ├─ 2. TURN ────────────────── needed for calls to work over mobile networks
#  ├─ 3. FEATURES ───────────── turn something ON (secret/certificate) or OFF
#  └─ 4. OPTIONAL ───────────── branding, access gates, fine-tuning
#
#  TWO DIRECTIONS, and this is the key point:
#    • Some variables turn a feature ON, because they supply the secret or certificate it
#      needs (EUDI_INVITE_SECRET, EUDI_PASS_*, EMBED_*). Without them the server does not
#      advertise the feature at all — the button never appears.
#    • EUDI_FEATURES_OFF turns OFF features that would otherwise be there. For operators
#      who must disable something for compliance reasons, not for licensing reasons.
#
#  What a running instance actually offers is always visible at:  GET /capabilities
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════


# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
# │ 1. REQUIRED                                                                         │
# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
# With NODE_ENV=production the server checks these values at startup and ABORTS if one is
# missing or still holds a placeholder — before the port is open. The reason: otherwise the
# mistake only surfaces when a citizen opens their wallet and gets a 401 back, and at that
# point nobody knows which value was missing. Messages always name the variable, never the
# value. See src/core/startup-checks.ts.

# The verifier — YOUR OWN EUDIPLO instance. This application ships none and cannot work
# without one. What that requires is described in the README (in short: EUDIPLO 8.0.2 with
# the version pinned, its own subdomain with TLS, an access certificate from a registration
# authority — the last of which is an application and takes weeks).
EUDIPLO_BASE_URL=https://verifier.example.org

# The tenant on your instance. The request appears in the wallet under ITS certificate —
# the tenant, not VERIFIER_CLIENT_ID, determines the visible identity. An instance running
# on someone else's tenant queries under someone else's registration; the server warns
# about that.
EUDIPLO_CLIENT_ID=your-tenant-id

# Fixed tenant secret. The recommended route: the application then holds only its tenant's
# rights (presentation:*), no `tenants:manage`, no root login, no rotation at runtime.
# Create or retrieve it in the EUDIPLO administration.
EUDIPLO_CLIENT_SECRET=__set_me__

# Public address of this instance. MAY BE OMITTED — and usually should be: without it the
# server derives the origin per request from x-forwarded-host/-proto, which works behind a
# reverse proxy and for several hostnames at once. Set it only if you need to pin it; a
# placeholder here breaks the wallet redirect back.
# PUBLIC_URL=https://call.example.org

# Port of the Node process. Behind a proxy it stays internal.
PORT=3000


# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
# │ 2. TURN — without this, calls over mobile networks will not connect                 │
# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
# On the same Wi-Fi a call works without a relay. As soon as one side is on a mobile
# network, the direct connection fails at the carrier NAT — that is when TURN is needed.
# The /ice endpoint hands the browser short-lived credentials so the secret never appears
# in the frontend. Without this configuration /ice falls back to public STUN servers, and
# calls over mobile networks fail silently.
#
# TURN_SECRET must be identical ON BOTH SIDES: here, and as `static-auth-secret` in the
# coturn configuration. Generate it with:  openssl rand -hex 32
# TURN_URL=turn:turn.example.org:3478
# TURN_SECRET=__openssl_rand_hex_32__
# TURN_TTL=86400

# TURN over 443, so guests on corporate and government networks can get through: there,
# often only 443/TCP is open. Requires a TLS router in front of coturn — see the README.
# TURN_TLS_URL=turns:turn.example.org:443?transport=tcp


# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
# │ 3. FEATURES — switching on or switching off                                         │
# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

# ── 3a) SWITCHING OFF ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Comma-separated list of feature IDs this instance should NOT offer. Intended for
# operators with compliance requirements: "file transfer during a citizen consultation:
# prohibited" is a realistic sentence from a data protection concept.
#
# GET /capabilities lists the valid IDs. An unknown name is reported at startup rather
# than silently ignored — a typo should be noticed.
#
# NOT switchable, deliberately: calls, eudi-verification (the product itself) as well as
# wrp-policy, pseudonym-id, audit-metadata (the privacy guarantees) and self-host. A
# guarantee the operator can switch off would not be a guarantee.
# EUDI_FEATURES_OFF=file-transfer,screen-share,qes-in-call

# ── 3b) SWITCHING ON: appointments and invitations ──────────────────────────────────────
# Signing secret for appointment invitations. WITHOUT it the instance cannot issue signed
# invitations (role + agreed scope + time window inside the link), and the `planned-invites`
# feature is not advertised — the client falls back to a plain room link instead of
# promising an appointment link that carries nothing after a restart.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# EUDI_INVITE_SECRET=__openssl_rand_hex_32__

# Appointment pass for Apple Wallet (.pkpass). Requires a pass certificate of your own from
# an Apple Developer account. Without these three values `wallet-pass` stays off.
# EUDI_PASS_TYPE_ID=pass.example.appointment
# EUDI_PASS_TEAM_ID=ABCDE12345
# EUDI_PASS_CERT=<base64 of certificate + key as PEM>

# ── 3c) SWITCHING ON: embedding in your own pages ───────────────────────────────────────
# Without either of these, framing stays DENIED — there is deliberately no wildcard default
# any more. Opening up framing is always an explicit act.
#   Option A: list concrete origins (open mode)
# EMBED_ALLOWED_ANCESTORS=https://portal.example.org https://office.example.org
#   Option B (hardened): signed tokens, framing only with a valid ?tok=
#   Generate a token with: node scripts/embed-token.mjs
# EMBED_SIGNING_SECRET=__openssl_rand_hex_32__

# ── 3d) SWITCHING ON: credentials during the call ───────────────────────────────────────
# Catalogue of further credentials that can be requested during an ongoing call. Without a
# catalogue file the feature stays off — it presupposes arrangements with the issuers.
# EUDI_CREDENTIAL_CATALOG=./credential-catalog.json
# EUDI_CREDENTIAL_REQUEST_TTL=300

# ── 3e) Qualified signatures ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Provider list for the signing ceremony. Without a file there is only the demo provider,
# with a permanent "not legally valid" notice. CSC/QTSP entries leave the server only when
# the `qes-csc` feature is active — commercially licensed, it needs EUDI_EDITION=pro AND the
# Pro module (EUDI_PRO_MODULE) — and presuppose a QTSP contract of your own.
# EUDI_SIGNATURE_PROVIDERS=./signature-providers.json

# ── 3f) Commercially licensed features ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `pro` unlocks the two features that have a real obligation behind them: the verification
# trail with retention and qualified remote signatures. Both are implemented in ./pro.
# Using them without a licence is a licensing question, not a technical one: the code stops
# nobody. See LICENSE-COMMERCIAL.md.
# EUDI_EDITION=community

# --- operator pages: imprint, privacy notice, press ------------------------------------------
# An imprint names whoever RUNS the service, so it belongs to the deployment, not to this
# software. Point this at a directory and put your own pages in it:
#
#   impressum.html · datenschutz.html · presse.html
#
# They are then served at /impressum, /datenschutz and /presse and take precedence over the
# neutral placeholders shipped here. Anything else in the directory (images, downloads) is served
# read-only under /operator/.
#
# Unset, nothing changes: the placeholders are served, which say plainly that the operator has not
# provided a page. That is the honest state — an imprint link that dead-ends is worse, and section
# 5 DDG requires the actual provider to be named, not the author of the software.
#
# A `start.html` in that directory takes over "/" — the operator's own front page. Without one,
# the bare URL opens the application itself, which is the honest default: this is a tool for
# making verified calls, not a website, and an instance you run should not greet visitors with
# someone else's marketing. Invitation links (`/?room=…`) always reach the application, start
# page or not.
#
# Anything else in the directory is served read-only under `/operator/` — a stylesheet for your
# pages, images, a film. `/capabilities` reports which of them exist, so a page can tell whether
# a file is there before pointing at it.
#
# In Docker: mount a volume and point this at it, so the pages survive a redeployment.
# EUDI_OPERATOR_PAGES=/data/pages


# Path to the Pro module. Relative paths are resolved from the working directory; `./pro` is
# the normal case. IF THIS VARIABLE IS SET AND THE MODULE FAILS TO LOAD, startup aborts in
# production — otherwise the instance would quietly carry on WITHOUT retention, and the
# evidence would be missing exactly when it is needed.
# EUDI_PRO_MODULE=./pro
# EUDI_AUDIT_FILE=/data/audit-log.jsonl

# Maximum number of participants in multi-party calls (mesh). With the feature switched off
# (EUDI_FEATURES_OFF=multi-party) it stays at 2. Realistic limits: video 4–5, audio 8–10, chat 15–20.
# EUDI_MAX_PARTICIPANTS=5

# Raises the EFFECTIVE edition to `pro` for everyone, with no account and no subscription
# gate. We use this during the launch phase of the hosted service so that everything can be
# tried out. Documented here for completeness, because the switch sits in the open code and
# shows up in /capabilities — the configured edition and the promo flag stay separately
# visible there, so nothing is being concealed. It changes nothing about licensing: it
# unlocks features, not rights of use.
# EUDI_PROMO_ALL_PRO=true


# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
# │ 4. OPTIONAL / ADVANCED                                                              │
# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

# ── Credential formats ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Which formats are offered. The default is mdoc ONLY, so that an update never silently
# changes the live request. `pid_mdoc,pid_sdjwt` enables both — only turn that on once your
# own instance handles `dc+sd-jwt` and DCQL `credential_sets`.
# A note from practice: the mdoc status list from the Bundesdruckerei was unreadable for
# EUDIPLO 6.2.0, while the SD-JWT path worked. When in doubt, SD-JWT.
# EUDI_CREDENTIAL_PROFILES=pid_mdoc,pid_sdjwt

# Trust lists against which the issuer of a presented PID is checked. DELIBERATELY without
# a default: whom an instance trusts is the operator's decision and must not come from the
# source code. The id of a list your EUDIPLO instance manages — the last path segment of
# https://<verifier>/issuers/<tenant>/trust-list/<id>. Replaces EUDI_TRUST_LIST_URLS, which
# EUDIPLO 7 and later no longer accept (startup refuses to run while it is still set).
# EUDI_TRUST_LIST_IDS=pid-issuers

# Doctype and vct of the PID. Only change these if a different set of issuers applies.
# PID_MDOC_DOCTYPE=eu.europa.ec.eudi.pid.1
# PID_SDJWT_VCT=urn:eudi:pid:de:1

# Channel binding: bind the verified identity to the DTLS key of THIS connection. OFF BY
# DEFAULT — wallets currently reject the as-yet-unknown transaction_data type, and rightly
# so. Effective only with SD-JWT VC.
# EUDI_CHANNEL_BINDING=on
# EUDI_CHANNEL_BINDING_TYPE=<a type the wallet supports>

# ── Registration and purpose limitation (eIDAS 2.0 / EU 2024/1183) ──────────────────────
# Selects one of the policies defined in src/wrp-policy.ts. The environment can therefore
# CHOOSE, but never extend — which attributes are permitted lives in the source code and is
# versioned. An unknown name aborts startup.
# EUDI_WRP_POLICY=kommune-buergerservice

# Ask for LESS than the selected policy permits. Comma-separated, and intersected with the policy
# — never unioned. That is the whole design: a deployment may narrow the request, and only a code
# change can widen it. A name the policy does not permit aborts startup rather than being ignored.
#
# Why you would: one instance, one customer, one purpose. A registration is drawn wide enough to
# cover every deployment under it; a single deployment covers one. A support line that only needs
# to know who is on the line should not ask for a date of birth merely because the registration
# would allow it — data minimisation is an obligation per PROCESSING, not per registration. That
# holds whoever runs the instance: an insurer, a notary, a clinic, a letting agent, an authority.
#
# /wrp-policy then publishes BOTH: what you ask (`allowedAttributes`) and what your registration
# permits (`registrationPermits`), so the narrowing is checkable from outside.
# EUDI_WRP_NARROW=given_name,family_name

# Enforcement: without `on`, requests outside the agreed scope are reduced to the allowlist
# and logged; with `on` they are rejected. Switch to `on` once your own clients are rolled
# out.
# EUDI_WRP_STRICT=on

# The PII-free verification record (timestamp, purpose, attribute TYPES, status; room and
# subject hashed) is written to stdout in every instance. `off` only silences that output.
# EUDI_AUDIT_STDOUT=off

# ── Access gates ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Only these people may act as host — checked after wallet verification against the
# verified name. Format: `Surname,Given name; Surname,Given name`. Without a list anyone may
# host. Its limit: the check can only establish that names match.
# EUDI_HOST_ALLOWLIST=Mustermann,Erika; Schmidt,Hans-Peter

# The same list as a FILE, re-read whenever it changes — no restart, and therefore no dropped
# calls. Prefer this wherever staff change: room state lives in the process, so a redeployment
# for a personnel change ends every call in progress. It also keeps personal data out of the
# deployment platform's UI, which otherwise widens who can read it to everyone with access there.
#
#   { "hosts": [ { "familyName": "Mustermann", "givenName": "Erika" } ] }
#
# A bare array works too, and an entry may be the "Family,Given" string the variable accepts.
# Both sources apply together. An unreadable or missing file admits NOBODY — it never opens the
# gate — and the instance says so at startup.
# EUDI_HOST_FILE=/data/hosts.json

# Staff token for POST /invite. Without a secret the endpoint is open — on a public-sector
# instance it should be set. Generate a link with: node scripts/mint-staff-link.mjs
# EUDI_STAFF_SECRET=__openssl_rand_hex_32__

# ── Branding ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Without these variables not a single byte of the delivered application changes. Purely
# cosmetic: the wallet/verifier identity remains that of the configured tenant.
# WHO IS ACTUALLY ASKING. Read this before branding anything.
#
# Branding changes the page, not the request. The wallet shows whoever owns the access
# certificate behind EUDIPLO_CLIENT_ID — EUDIPLO derives client_id from that certificate, so no
# variable here can change it. On a branded instance running on someone else's tenant, the person
# being asked sees one organisation on screen and a different one in their wallet.
#
# That gap is not hidden, it is stated: the verification dialogue names the actual relying party
# above the QR code, before anyone opens a wallet. Set this to the LEGAL ENTITY behind your
# tenant — not a domain, not a tenant id — so the sentence can name them:
#
#     "Die Ausweisabfrage stellt Kontrollfeld GmbH im Auftrag von Beispiel Kundenservice."
#
# Leave it unset and the sentence still appears, in its blunter form ("...stellt nicht Beispiel
# Kundenservice, sondern der Betreiber dieser Instanz"). There is no setting that removes it. It
# disappears when there is nothing left to disclose: your own tenant, your own certificate, this
# name equal to EUDI_BRAND_NAME.
# EUDI_RP_NAME=Kontrollfeld GmbH

# EUDI_BRAND_NAME=Beispiel Kundenservice
# EUDI_BRAND_ACCENT=#0369a1
# EUDI_BRAND_ACCENT_DARK=#38bdf8
# EUDI_BRAND_LOGO=/brand/logo.svg

# ── Network and clients ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Permitted WebSocket origins (protection against cross-site WebSocket hijacking). Without
# a value the heuristic "origin host == host" applies, which is usually right behind a
# proxy.
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://call.example.org

# Additional origins for native app shells (Capacitor). Set to "" to reject app clients.
# EUDI_APP_ORIGINS=capacitor://localhost,https://localhost,ionic://localhost

# Universal Links / App Links, so that ?room= links open the installed app.
# EUDI_IOS_APP_ID=<TeamID>.<BundleID>
# EUDI_ANDROID_PACKAGE=com.example.app
# EUDI_ANDROID_SHA256=<SHA-256 of the app signing certificate from the Play Console>

# ── Miscellaneous ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Legacy route for a SHARED EUDIPLO instance whose tenant secret you do not know
# (root login → rotate-secret → tenant token). Ignored as soon as EUDIPLO_CLIENT_SECRET is
# set. Not needed for an instance of your own.
# EUDIPLO_ROOT_ID=your-root-id
# EUDIPLO_ROOT_SECRET=__set_me__
